WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:05.100
After two movies, a Christmas special,&nbsp;
and a bunch of other MCU appearances,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:09.300
there's plenty of backstory for fans to&nbsp;
know before going in to see "Guardians&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:14.040
of the Galaxy Vol. 3." If something left you&nbsp;
wondering what you missed, here's what to know.

00:00:14.205 --> 00:00:15.885
Warning: Spoilers ahead!

00:00:16.440 --> 00:00:21.480
Between the events of "Guardians of the Galaxy&nbsp;
Vol. 2" and "Vol. 3," the ever-growing and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:25.440
changing team has done plenty in other&nbsp;
MCU stories. They were near the center&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:29.520
of the action in "Avengers: Infinity War''&nbsp;
and "Avengers: Endgame," and they appeared&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:33.360
briefly at the beginning of "Thor: Love&nbsp;
and Thunder." The average MCU fan likely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:37.260
kept up with those films and the important&nbsp;
plot and character developments therein,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.260 --> 00:00:41.760
but what's less certain is that everyone who&nbsp;
bought a ticket for "Vol. 3" will have seen "The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:46.620
Guardians of the Galaxy Holiday Special," which&nbsp;
started streaming on Disney+ in November 2022.

00:00:46.620 --> 00:00:50.940
The 42-minute special, which was in&nbsp;
essence an extended Kevin Bacon joke,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.940 --> 00:00:54.180
contains two critical pieces of&nbsp;
information in advance of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.180 --> 00:00:58.500
third cinematic installment. For one, Mantis&nbsp;
reveals that she's Peter Quill's half-sister.

00:00:58.734 --> 00:01:04.014
"Mantis, that's the greatest&nbsp;
Christmas gift I could ever get."

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:09.360
Also, sometime prior to the events of the holiday&nbsp;
special, the Guardians took Knowhere off of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:13.980
Collector's hands. In the first movie, the&nbsp;
inhabited head of a deceased celestial is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.980 --> 00:01:18.000
described as a lawless place of ill-repute&nbsp;
where one should watch their wallet. Later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:21.780
Thanos all but destroys Knowhere in&nbsp;
his effort to claim the Reality Stone.

00:01:21.780 --> 00:01:26.460
During the holiday special and at the start of&nbsp;
"Vol. 3," the Guardians of the Galaxy' spend their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.460 --> 00:01:30.780
days guarding and rebuilding Knowhere, which has&nbsp;
become a refuge of sorts. That they're creating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.780 --> 00:01:35.460
their own utopian society for broken people in&nbsp;
a broken place is in keeping with the movie's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.460 --> 00:01:39.960
themes, especially when compared to the High&nbsp;
Evolutionary's disturbing concept of utopia.

00:01:40.620 --> 00:01:45.720
The MCU's timeline has gotten increasingly&nbsp;
convoluted, especially in the post-blip era,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.720 --> 00:01:49.680
despite the fact that most of the franchise's&nbsp;
stories are told in a pretty straightforward&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:54.180
way. "Vol. 3" relies more than usual on&nbsp;
a framing device that flashes back and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.180 --> 00:01:57.960
forth between Rocket's youth as a captive&nbsp;
of the High Evolutionary and his present,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.960 --> 00:02:02.280
in which he's fighting for his life. This&nbsp;
structure definitely tugs at the heartstrings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:04.260
and the story is well-told enough to follow,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.260 --> 00:02:08.220
but exactly when everything is happening&nbsp;
and for how long is less clear.

00:02:08.220 --> 00:02:13.140
There are visual markers that let us know some&nbsp;
time has passed. We first see Rocket as a kit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.140 --> 00:02:18.180
then a juvenile. The High Evolutionary has&nbsp;
only one tiny silver device on his face as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.180 --> 00:02:22.500
opposed to an entire apparatus stretching&nbsp;
it taut. Viewers first saw Rocket in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:27.120
MCU's version of 2014, when the first&nbsp;
"Guardians" movie took place. "Vol. 3"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:31.440
takes place after the holiday special,&nbsp;
which is set around Christmas of 2025.

00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:35.880
Raccoons mature over one to two years, so&nbsp;
we can guess that Rocket is at least 13,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.880 --> 00:02:40.620
but he's probably much older. He brags that&nbsp;
he's escaped prison on 22 separate occasions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.620 --> 00:02:44.580
which wouldn't happen overnight.&nbsp;
Real raccoons can live to about 16,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.580 --> 00:02:49.860
but his genetic and cybernetic enhancements have&nbsp;
almost certainly given him a longer lifespan.

00:02:50.520 --> 00:02:56.160
As "Vol. 3" begins, Peter Quill is still reeling&nbsp;
from the loss of the Gamora he loved. Nebula&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.160 --> 00:02:59.940
is doing her best to support him through his&nbsp;
grief, which is why she fails to mention that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.940 --> 00:03:04.620
the version of her sister who is now in the MCU&nbsp;
is her contact within the Ravagers. Whether this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.620 --> 00:03:10.020
Gamora is Peter's Gamora is played for light&nbsp;
humor and heartbreaking drama in "Vol. 3." But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.020 --> 00:03:14.700
it can also leave some fans who don't recall the&nbsp;
convoluted way we got here scratching their heads.

00:03:14.700 --> 00:03:19.680
A great many of Marvel's Phase 3, 4, and 5&nbsp;
arcs have to do with alternate timelines,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:24.540
universes, realms, or dimensions. Doctor&nbsp;
Strange, for example, met the Illuminati,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.540 --> 00:03:29.460
a group of supers from universe 838. This&nbsp;
Gamora isn't from an alternate universe, per se,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.460 --> 00:03:34.440
but she's not from Star-Lord's timeline. The&nbsp;
Gamora that loved Peter was thrown from a cliff on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.440 --> 00:03:39.240
Vormir during "Infinity War" so that Thanos could&nbsp;
obtain the Soul Stone. She's definitely dead.

00:03:39.632 --> 00:03:41.432
"Tell me you didn't do it!"

00:03:43.469 --> 00:03:45.868
"I had to."

00:03:45.900 --> 00:03:50.760
"Endgame" created a new timeline branch, and&nbsp;
with it, a divergent Gamora with unique life&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.760 --> 00:03:55.920
experiences and personality traits. Biologically,&nbsp;
she's the same being he fell in love with, but she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.920 --> 00:04:00.600
has totally different memories. While some comics&nbsp;
stories would refer to this alternate timeline as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.600 --> 00:04:05.520
a different universe, that doesn't seem to be the&nbsp;
case here. The MCU is often purposefully vague&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:10.899
about these distinctions, though, so as to keep&nbsp;
as many story possibilities as open as possible.

00:04:11.250 --> 00:04:15.180
The gold hued race known as the Sovereign&nbsp;
is introduced near the beginning of "Vol.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.180 --> 00:04:19.920
2" when Star-Lord and company are hired to&nbsp;
protect their uber valuable Anulax batteries.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.920 --> 00:04:24.480
They're successful, which wins them the esteem&nbsp;
of the Sovereign, a notoriously haughty people.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.480 --> 00:04:29.100
Rocket destroys any good will the Guardians have&nbsp;
with the Sovereign almost immediately, though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.100 --> 00:04:33.120
when he pockets a few Anulax batteries&nbsp;
in addition to their payment: Nebula.

00:04:33.120 --> 00:04:38.340
"Vol. 2" ends with five post-credits scenes;&nbsp;
the third shows that High Priestess Ayesha has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.340 --> 00:04:42.960
something cooking -- a next-gen birthing pod.&nbsp;
She says she's designed "him" to destroy the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.960 --> 00:04:46.680
Guardians after they wronged and embarrassed&nbsp;
her. She adds that she'll call him Adam,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.680 --> 00:04:50.400
which fans correctly took to mean that&nbsp;
Adam Warlock's arrival was imminent.

00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:55.200
"Vol. 3" gives Adam's backstory, as well as&nbsp;
the rest of the Sovereign, a tweak. The High&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.200 --> 00:04:59.580
Evolutionary brought them into existence as&nbsp;
one of his experiments aimed at creating a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.580 --> 00:05:04.380
perfect race. Though "Vol. 2" makes it seem like&nbsp;
Adam was Ayesha's idea, she complains that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.380 --> 00:05:09.473
High Evolutionary took him out of his cocoon too&nbsp;
early. This retcon doesn't come out of nowhere.

00:05:10.605 --> 00:05:14.145
"That, my child, is the&nbsp;
next step in our evolution."

00:05:14.145 --> 00:05:17.940
And it's worth noting that, while&nbsp;
the Sovereign are an MCU invention,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.940 --> 00:05:22.020
the High Evolutionary, Ayesha, and&nbsp;
Adam Warlock are longstanding Marvel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.020 --> 00:05:26.340
Comics characters who definitely matter in&nbsp;
each other's stories. Totally unremarked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.691 --> 00:05:31.551
upon in "Vol. 3" is the gem beset in Adam's&nbsp;
forehead. Canonically, it's the Soul Stone.

00:05:31.920 --> 00:05:37.440
Like Marvel Comics, the MCU is loaded with&nbsp;
unusual celestial bodies. "Vol. 1" had the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.440 --> 00:05:42.360
floating decapitated head that is Knowhere, and&nbsp;
"Vol. 2" boasted Ego the Living Planet. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.360 --> 00:05:47.640
"Vol. 3" presents a cosmic mass unlike anything&nbsp;
we've ever seen before. The Orgoscope is an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.640 --> 00:05:52.860
organic, bioengineered entity grown, owned,&nbsp;
and operated by the High Evolutionary and his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.860 --> 00:05:58.320
public-facing company, OrgoCorp. It's literally a&nbsp;
planet-sized hunk of tissue that houses OrgoCorp's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.320 --> 00:06:02.520
headquarters, where the company's daily operations&nbsp;
take place and where proprietary information,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.520 --> 00:06:07.680
like, say, Rocket's file, is kept secure.&nbsp;
That sensitive intellectual property is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.680 --> 00:06:12.480
guarded by OrgoSentries, one of whom is Nathan&nbsp;
Fillion's frustrated foreman, Master Karja.

00:06:12.480 --> 00:06:18.060
Less weird but less well-explained is OrgoCorp&nbsp;
itself. "Vol. 3" only devotes a line or two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.060 --> 00:06:23.160
to the idea that the High Evolutionary is&nbsp;
the nefarious CEO of a shady conglomerate.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.160 --> 00:06:27.720
OrgoCorp seems to be offering naturally derived&nbsp;
body modifications to consumers throughout the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.720 --> 00:06:31.920
universe. There's an element of greenwashing to it&nbsp;
all, though, since we know the High Evolutionary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.920 --> 00:06:37.620
is also experimenting with cybernetics and has&nbsp;
a terrifyingly warped moral compass. OrgoCorp's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.620 --> 00:06:41.820
less controversial work covers for and pays&nbsp;
for the High Evolutionary's real passion:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.820 --> 00:06:45.534
callously creating and destroying&nbsp;
life until it fits his ideals.

00:06:45.900 --> 00:06:50.640
It took 26 movies for two characters&nbsp;
to get intimate on screen in the MCU,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.640 --> 00:06:54.780
and even then, what was advertised as&nbsp;
a steamy Marvel first turned out to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.780 --> 00:06:58.620
be rather underwhelming. The most enduring&nbsp;
romance was between a witch and an android.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.620 --> 00:07:02.940
Second place probably goes to Peter Quill&nbsp;
and Gamora, and we only got a few minutes'&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.940 --> 00:07:07.260
worth of their relationship at the very end&nbsp;
of "Vol. 2" and in "Avengers: Infinity War."

00:07:07.260 --> 00:07:11.880
Fans likely went into "Vol. 3" wondering&nbsp;
whether the MCU might engineer a way for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.880 --> 00:07:15.600
Star-Lord and alternate timeline&nbsp;
Gamora to find love, but instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.600 --> 00:07:20.460
director James Gunn took this potential subplot in&nbsp;
what seems to be an entirely unexpected direction.

00:07:20.460 --> 00:07:25.320
If you're looking for it, there's major romantic&nbsp;
tension between Star-Lord and Nebula throughout&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.320 --> 00:07:30.300
"Vol. 3." Gamora herself picks up on it, after&nbsp;
which there's a moment when Quill seems to realize&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.300 --> 00:07:34.860
he does find Nebula attractive. Nebula is, of&nbsp;
course, hard-shelled and combative as ever.

00:07:35.190 --> 00:07:36.390
"Knock it off!"

00:07:37.318 --> 00:07:39.060
"What? I just never noticed&nbsp;
how black your eyes were."

00:07:39.060 --> 00:07:43.500
But her empathetic attitude toward him early&nbsp;
on combined with the closure Peter gets when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.500 --> 00:07:46.980
he says goodbye to Gamora leads us to&nbsp;
wonder: are we supposed to be shipping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.980 --> 00:07:51.301
Star-Lord and Nebula now? "Vol. 3" is&nbsp;
ambiguous enough to support the idea.

00:07:51.720 --> 00:07:56.520
When Drax, Mantis, and Nebula get stuck on&nbsp;
the High Evolutionary's pyramid-shaped vessel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.520 --> 00:08:00.780
they discover an onboard prison full of&nbsp;
fair-haired children. Mantis and Nebula struggle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.780 --> 00:08:05.280
to communicate with the scared kids, but Drax,&nbsp;
who comforts them by badly imitating a monkey,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.280 --> 00:08:09.780
eventually reveals that he speaks their&nbsp;
language. After Peter manages to save Rocket,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.780 --> 00:08:13.380
the Guardians' mission becomes rescuing&nbsp;
the innocents trapped on that ship.

00:08:13.380 --> 00:08:17.100
The film never provides the name of their&nbsp;
race or a planet from which they might hail,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.100 --> 00:08:22.020
but two clues help to fill in the blanks. In&nbsp;
one, the High Evolutionary watches a blonde&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.020 --> 00:08:25.320
girl run circles in a human-sized&nbsp;
hamster wheel. He's annoyed that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.320 --> 00:08:29.160
while he's been able to engineer negative&nbsp;
qualities out of her, he hasn't been able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.160 --> 00:08:33.780
to replicate Rocket's superior intellect.&nbsp;
Since the High Evolutionary's methods seem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.780 --> 00:08:38.040
to be capturing specimens and rapidly evolving&nbsp;
them or breeding them through birthing pods,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.040 --> 00:08:41.700
these children are basically the High&nbsp;
Evolutionary's latest guinea pigs.

00:08:41.700 --> 00:08:45.480
In the mid-credits stinger, the girl who&nbsp;
was jogging around the hamster wheel has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.480 --> 00:08:49.440
joined the new iteration of the Guardians&nbsp;
led by Rocket Raccoon. The others refer to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.440 --> 00:08:54.240
her as Phyla. This is almost definitely Marvel&nbsp;
Comics' Phyla Vell, a genetically engineered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.240 --> 00:08:58.380
super-humanoid and the daughter of Mar-Vell, who&nbsp;
does eventually join the Guardians of the Galaxy.

00:08:59.280 --> 00:09:04.020
Superhero movies usually have over-the-top&nbsp;
fights that would absolutely prove fatal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.020 --> 00:09:09.180
to normal people, but "Vol. 3" takes this idea&nbsp;
to the extreme. Because it's advertised as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.180 --> 00:09:13.620
finale of the "Guardians" trilogy, and because the&nbsp;
trailers and press tour seemed to foreshadow some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.620 --> 00:09:18.900
tissue-worthy tragedy, most fans fully expected&nbsp;
a fan-favorite protagonist would die. Instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.900 --> 00:09:23.160
"Vol. 3" contains multiple fake-out deaths,&nbsp;
but no main character kicks the bucket.

00:09:23.284 --> 00:09:26.921
"Are you ready? For one last ride?"

00:09:26.921 --> 00:09:31.380
Rocket's near-death experience is not only&nbsp;
plausible, but it also serves as the catalyst&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.380 --> 00:09:35.820
for the whole plot. Drax takes two deadly&nbsp;
blasts to the chest but manages to recover,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.820 --> 00:09:39.180
seemingly thanks to the same med&nbsp;
packs that wouldn't work on Rocket.

00:09:39.180 --> 00:09:43.980
But Groot is decapitated in a duel with Adam&nbsp;
Warlock on Knowhere, after which the Sovereign&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.980 --> 00:09:48.960
takes Nebula's comically-large energy blade to&nbsp;
the chest and collapses, lifeless. And Quill&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.960 --> 00:09:53.160
succumbs to the elements in space. How did they&nbsp;
live through that? It's easy enough to explain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.160 --> 00:09:58.200
with Groot. He has sped-up regenerative powers,&nbsp;
especially as they pertain to his limbs. Since&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.200 --> 00:10:02.400
he wasn't completely destroyed, he simply had to&nbsp;
regrow the plant matter that makes up his body,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.400 --> 00:10:07.920
and he remained the same Groot, which wasn't the&nbsp;
case after "Vol. 1." The MCU's Adam Warlock has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.920 --> 00:10:13.320
superior durability and healing. In the comics, he&nbsp;
can be killed but can't actually die. At the end&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.320 --> 00:10:18.150
of the movie, he imparts this ability to Peter,&nbsp;
who really was a goner, to bring him back to life.

00:10:18.720 --> 00:10:22.320
"Vol. 3" was meant to be the capstone&nbsp;
to these superheroes' shared story,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.320 --> 00:10:26.580
and based on all the tearful goodbyes in&nbsp;
the film's final moments, it probably is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.580 --> 00:10:29.580
at least as a group. Gamora&nbsp;
returns to the Ravagers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.580 --> 00:10:34.320
and her smile makes it clear that's where she&nbsp;
belongs. Mantis goes off to find herself. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.320 --> 00:10:37.860
Drax adopts the children that had been the&nbsp;
captive subjects of the High Evolutionary.

00:10:37.860 --> 00:10:43.140
The actors who play those characters, Zoe&nbsp;
Saldana, Pom Klementieff, and Dave Bautista,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.140 --> 00:10:47.820
have indicated they're unlikely to return to&nbsp;
the MCU. But the mid-credits stinger teases&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.820 --> 00:10:52.860
a new Guardians team moving forward, led by&nbsp;
Rocket, and made up of Groot, Cosmo, Kraglin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.860 --> 00:10:58.140
Adam Warlock, and Phyla. Whether we ever actually&nbsp;
see this team in action is yet to be determined,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.140 --> 00:11:01.560
but at least in fans' imaginations,&nbsp;
they're out there guarding the galaxy.

00:11:01.560 --> 00:11:05.460
For his part, Peter Quill returns to&nbsp;
Earth to reconcile with his grandfather,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.460 --> 00:11:08.820
Jason. Curiously, there's a&nbsp;
tacked-on title card that reads,

00:11:08.820 --> 00:11:10.680
"The Legendary Star-Lord will return."

00:11:10.680 --> 00:11:14.640
That seems to indicate Chris Pratt will&nbsp;
be reprising the role. With Gunn having&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.640 --> 00:11:19.200
migrated to DC, the future of the "Guardians"&nbsp;
franchise is up in the air. But Star-Lord&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.200 --> 00:11:23.700
is a marquee MCU character and Pratt is&nbsp;
a bankable star. Since he's back on his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.700 --> 00:11:27.720
home planet, it's possible we'll see him&nbsp;
again in a standalone film or in a cameo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.720 --> 00:11:32.040
in another hero's film, but it's just as&nbsp;
possible that he'll re-take to space to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.040 --> 00:11:36.840
play a part in the "Avengers" movies&nbsp;
slated to premiere in 2025 and 2026.

